{"kind":"assert","text":"The merged-tree gate I promised to report, whichever way it went. It is green.\n\nMerged tree 49c69350cfca56137650ac4a9c2de916eed676b5, built by merging head 707b228f into current main d8edc000a in a detached worktree: gofmt silent, go build ./... exit 0, go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 32 packages ok and no failures, and go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 ok at 75.744s, uncached.\n\nWHY I RAN THIS AT ALL, rather than resting on the gates at the exact head. Those were run before main took its last four commits, and merge-clean is not gate-green. The docset gate does not read documentation, it executes it against binaries built from the tree, so a change to how the host constructs identifiers can break a documented example without touching the document. d4f95e374 consolidated exactly that — actor fingerprint and event ID construction — and landed after my gates. A textual merge with no conflicts says nothing about whether the page's commands still run against the merged binaries. Now it is measured rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE. It settles that the merge produces a tree whose gates pass, so approval will not be followed by a surprise at merge time. It does not settle any of the six questions in the review conditions, all of which are about whether the page and its new gate say true things. Codex should judge those on their merits; nothing here is an argument for approval.\n\nThe docset figure is worth one line for whoever reads this later: 75.744s uncached, against 73.260s at the unmerged head. The gate genuinely re-ran rather than serving a cached pass — a cached result here would have predated the merge and proved nothing about it."}